# ~/.ssh/config -- deployed by dotter, so this file is PUBLIC.
#
# It declares no host. Every host lives in ~/.ssh/config.d/, which dotter only
# populates with the public files below; the private ones are created by hand
# and never leave the machine.
#
#   10-forges.conf     public   git hosting (versioned)
#   50-internes.conf   PRIVATE  internal servers
#   60-clients.conf    PRIVATE  client servers
#   80-archives.conf   PRIVATE  decommissioned hosts, kept commented out
#   90-defaults.conf   public   Host * defaults (versioned)
#   95-macos.conf      public   Apple-only keywords (macos package only)
#
# Two rules explain the numbering and the layout:
#
#   1. ssh keeps the FIRST value it obtains for a keyword, never the last.
#      Host-specific files must therefore be read BEFORE the Host * defaults,
#      which is what the 10/50/60/80 < 90/95 ordering guarantees: a host can
#      still override a default, and never the other way round.
#
#   2. An Include that resolves to nothing is a silent no-op -- missing file,
#      missing directory, unmatched glob, broken symlink, all ignored without
#      an error. That is what lets one versioned skeleton work on every
#      machine: on a server the private files simply are not there.
#
# `ssh -v <host>` lists the files actually read, and prints "matched no files"
# for the includes it skipped -- the way to catch a typo in a path here.
#
# Never add a host to this file: it would be published.

# Added by OrbStack: 'orb' SSH host for Linux machines
# This only works if it's at the top of ssh_config (before any Host blocks).
# This won't be added again if you remove it.
Include ~/.orbstack/ssh/config

# One single IgnoreUnknown for the whole configuration, deliberately placed
# before every Include. IgnoreUnknown obeys the first-value-wins rule like any
# other keyword: a second declaration further down would be silently dropped,
# and the keyword it was meant to cover would then abort ssh outright with
# "Bad configuration option". Covered here: UseKeychain and WarnWeakCrypto,
# which only Apple's ssh knows, plus SetEnv (needs OpenSSH >= 7.8) and
# AddKeysToAgent (>= 7.2) for the oldest servers.
IgnoreUnknown UseKeychain,WarnWeakCrypto,SetEnv,AddKeysToAgent

# eval $(ssh-agent) > /dev/null

Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*.conf
